## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of graph algorithms, state expansion techniques, and path reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm on expanded state space (node, used)
- Edge relaxation with and without discount application
- Path reconstruction across state transitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithDiscounts(n, edges, s, t, K) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>[]);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(null));

  const pq = new MinHeap();
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.insert([s,0,0]); // [node, used, distance]

  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [u,used,d] = pq.extractMin();
    if(d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if(u === t) break;

    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      // Without discount
      if(d + w < dist[v][used]){
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used,d+w]);
      }
      // With discount if available
      if(used < K && d < dist[v][used+1]){
        dist[v][used+1] = d;
        prev[v][used+1] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used+1,d]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Find optimal state at target
  let minDist = INF, minUsed = -1;
  for(let k=0;k<=K;k++){
    if(dist[t][k] < minDist){
      minDist = dist[t][k];
      minUsed = k;
    }
  }

  // Path reconstruction
  const path = [];
  let curr = [t,minUsed];
  while(curr && prev[curr[0]][curr[1]]){
    path.unshift(curr[0]);
    curr = prev[curr[0]][curr[1]];
  }
  if(curr[0] === s) path.unshift(s);

  return {distance: minDist, path};
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Unreachable target: return INF distance
- K=0: reduces to standard Dijkstra
- K larger than path length: effectively unlimited discounts
- Multiple optimal states: choose minimal used count

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to blend BFS intuition with shortest-path techniques under a hop constraint and to reconstruct a path from augmented state.

## Key Concepts
- Augmented state graph: (node, hops)
- Dijkstra with 2D dist and predecessor tracking
- Path reconstruction from (node, hops) chain
- Time/space: O((N*H + M*H) log(N*H))

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathWithHops(graph, S, T, H) {
  // graph: {u: [{to,w}, ...], ...}
  const dist = new Map();
  const prev = new Map();
  // min-heap assumed available: push({time, node, hops})
  const heap = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.time-b.time);
  dist.set(`${S}|0`, 0);
  heap.push({time:0, node:S, hops:0});
  while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    const {time,u,k} = heap.pop();
    if (time !== dist.get(`${u}|${k}`)) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of (graph[u] || [])) {
      const nk = k + 1;
      if (nk > H) continue;
      const nd = time + w;
      const key = `${v}|${nk}`;
      if (nd < (dist.get(key) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(key, nd);
        prev.set(key, `${u}|${k}`);
        heap.push({time: nd, node: v, hops: nk});
      }
    }
  }
  let bestTime = Infinity; let bestKey = null;
  for (let k = 0; k <= H; k++) {
    const key = `${T}|${k}`;
    const d = dist.get(key);
    if (d != null && d < bestTime) { bestTime = d; bestKey = key; }
  }
  const path = [];
  if (bestKey) {
    let cur = bestKey;
    while (cur) {
      const [node] = cur.split('|');
      path.push(node);
      cur = prev.get(cur);
    }
    path.reverse();
  }
  return { time: bestTime, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple equal shortest paths?
- How would you optimize memory for very large graphs?
- How would you adapt if hops constraint is on edges vs. nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design both memoized and tabulated DP for a non-trivial partition constraint, reinforcing DP patterns and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with arrays
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Power-of-two check with bit trick

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPartitions(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const isPow2 = (x)=> x>0 && (x&(x-1))===0;
  // Memoized top-down
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const top = (i)=>{
    if(i===n) return 1;
    if(memo[i]!==-1) return memo[i];
    let res = 0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) res += top(j);
    }
    memo[i]=res; return res;
  };
  // Bottom-up
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n]=1;
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    let sumWays=0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) sumWays += dp[j];
    }
    dp[i]=sumWays;
  }
  return {topWays: top(0), bottomWays: dp[0]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine arrays/linked lists (deques), hash maps, and a heap to maintain a sliding-window top-K efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window, expiration, lazy deletions in a heap
- Per-user state via linked-list/deque and running sums
- Time/space trade-offs for real-time top-K

## Code Example
```javascript
class WaveTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.userMap = new Map(); // user_id -> {queue: [], sum}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // stores [sum, user_id]
  }
  addEvent(userId, score, ts) {
    let entry = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!entry) {
      entry = { queue: [], sum: 0 };
      this.userMap.set(userId, entry);
    }
    entry.queue.push([ts, score]);
    entry.sum += score;
    this.heap.push([entry.sum, userId]);
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const seen = new Map();
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [sum, uid] = this.heap.pop();
      const ent = this.userMap.get(uid);
      if (!ent) continue;
      // expire check
      this.expireUser(uid, currentTime);
      if (ent.sum !== sum) {
        this.heap.push([ent.sum, uid]);
        continue;
      }
      res.push(uid);
    }
    // Reinsert popped elements
    for (const uid of res) this.heap.push([this.userMap.get(uid).sum, uid]);
    return res;
  }
  expireUser(userId, currentTime) {
    const ent = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!ent) return;
    while (ent.queue.length && ent.queue[0][0] <= currentTime - this.W) {
      const [, val] = ent.queue.shift();
      ent.sum -= val;
    }
  }
}
```

